---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="weight-converter">
  <UnitConverter client:load dimension="weight" from="kg" to="lb" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert a weight</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Enter your figure in either box. Both fields update, so the weight converter works for kilograms to pounds and pounds to kilograms.</li>
      <li>Choose your units from the grouped list: metric, imperial and US, or the precious-metal units at the bottom.</li>
      <li>Read the result, the formula, and the two-way chart below, which is useful when you need a row of values and not one number.</li>
      <li>Copy the result with one click, or share the URL, which encodes the unit pair you chose.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Reading UK, US and metric body weight</h2>
    <p>
      Body weight is where the three systems meet most often. The UK uses stone and pounds ("11 st 4"), the US
      uses pounds ("158 lb"), and almost everywhere else uses kilograms. A stone is exactly 14 pounds, so 11 st
      4 lb is 158 lb, or 71.7 kg. The converter treats stone as a decimal unit: 11.5 st means eleven and a half
      stone, which is 11 st 7 lb, not 11 st 5 lb.
    </p>
    <p>
      For recipes, shipping and gym plates, remember that 1 kg is about 2.2 lb, a 20 kg plate is 44 lb, and a
      US 45 lb plate is 20.4 kg. People usually treat the two plates as the same, although they differ.
    </p>

    <h2>Which factors are exact</h2>
    <p>
      Since the 1959 international yard and pound agreement, the pound is exactly 0.45359237 kilograms. The
      imperial units follow from it without rounding: an ounce is exactly one sixteenth of a pound, a stone
      exactly fourteen pounds, a US short ton exactly 2,000 pounds and a UK long ton exactly 2,240. The metric
      tonne is exactly 1,000 kg.
    </p>
    <p>
      The troy ounce, at 31.1034768 g, is different. It is used only for gold, silver and platinum. A gold price
      per "ounce" applied to the everyday 28.35 g ounce understates the metal by just under 10 %, a costly
      error.
    </p>

    <h2>Common weights in kilograms, pounds and stone</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Kilograms</th><th>Pounds</th><th>Stone and pounds</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>50 kg</td><td>110.2 lb</td><td>7 st 12 lb</td></tr>
          <tr><td>60 kg</td><td>132.3 lb</td><td>9 st 6 lb</td></tr>
          <tr><td>70 kg</td><td>154.3 lb</td><td>11 st 0 lb</td></tr>
          <tr><td>80 kg</td><td>176.4 lb</td><td>12 st 8 lb</td></tr>
          <tr><td>90 kg</td><td>198.4 lb</td><td>14 st 2 lb</td></tr>
          <tr><td>100 kg</td><td>220.5 lb</td><td>15 st 10 lb</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Mass and weight</h2>
    <p>
      In physics, mass is the amount of matter in an object, measured in kilograms; weight is the force of
      gravity on that mass, measured in newtons. A 70 kg person weighs about 687 N on Earth and about 114 N on
      the Moon, and their mass is 70 kg in both places.
    </p>
    <p>
      In practice, every scale is calibrated for Earth's gravity and shows mass, and every unit on this page
      (kilogram, pound, ounce, stone, ton) is a mass unit. So the numbers from a "weight converter" are the
      ones you want.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
